          "text": "{\"target_identification\":{\"speaker\":\"Unnamed first-person narrator\",\"addressee\":\"Maria\",\"anchor_quote\":\"Saavist som jeg altid hellere har sagt Dig for lidt\\nend for meget, saavist elsker jeg Dig. Jeg elsker Dig i Lyst og i\\nNød, Hverdage og Festdage. Du er den, jeg vil dø med, men først vil\\njeg leve meget, meget længe med Dig. Javel har jeg længtes efter\\nmin yndige Elskerindes Kærtegn. Men mest har jeg længtes efter alt\\ndet andet, som ogsaa er Dig: dit trofaste Hjærte, din rene Tanke,\\ndin milde Tale, dit lyse Smil, som er min Sol i Tvivlen, dit gode\\nHaandtryk, som er min Styrke paa Vejen.\"},\"target_translation\":\"I love you.\",\"dimensions\":{\"T\":{\"score\":4,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.99,\"reason\":\"The focal words directly avow the narrator's present love for Maria. Their oath-like framing emphasizes that he presents this avowal as assuredly true, especially in response to her fear that his feeling may be temporary.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"elsker jeg Dig\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Saavist som jeg altid hellere har sagt Dig for lidt\\nend for meget, saavist elsker jeg Dig.\"}]},\"P\":{\"score\":3,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.92,\"reason\":\"The occurrence substantially affirms an enduring relational undertaking, not merely a momentary feeling. Maria has asked whether this is something he will regret tomorrow, and the oath-framed focal avowal is immediately specified as love through joy, hardship, ordinary days and celebrations, together with a wish for a long shared life. Those specifications establish the focal avowal itself as an assurance of constancy, rather than being only contextual promises unrelated to it. Its dominant force nevertheless remains an avowal, and the practical terms of the undertaking are not fully specified.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"For det er jo sandt, ikke noget, Du imorgen fortryder, ikke\\nnoget, Du nu bilder Dig ind, fordi Du har længtes en Smule efter din\\nElskerinde?\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Saavist som jeg altid hellere har sagt Dig for lidt\\nend for meget, saavist elsker jeg Dig. Jeg elsker Dig i Lyst og i\\nNød, Hverdage og Festdage. Du er den, jeg vil dø med, men først vil\\njeg leve meget, meget længe med Dig.\"}]},\"E\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.9,\"reason\":\"Although the reunion is highly emotional, the focal expression is a composed and reasoned answer to Maria's question, followed by an explanation of the scope and grounds of his love. It is not represented as a reflex-like affective discharge.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Jeg svarer: »Saavist som jeg altid hellere har sagt Dig for lidt\\nend for meget, saavist elsker jeg Dig.\"}]},\"O\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.91,\"reason\":\"The occurrence's core force is adequately captured by the truthful-state avowal and its contextually established affirmation of an enduring bond. No distinct residual speech-act function is needed.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"elsker jeg Dig\"}]}},\"original_speech_situation\":\"At an emotional reunion, Maria asks the unnamed narrator whether his changed attitude is genuine, lasting, and not something he will regret the next day. He answers her directly with the focal love avowal, solemnly frames it as certain, and elaborates that his love encompasses hardship, ordinary life, celebration, and a long life together.\",\"affection_evidence\":\"present\",\"undertaking_and_uptake\":\"The focal avowal affirms constancy in a shared bond: he positions himself as loving Maria through both pleasure and need and as intending a long life with her. Maria's concern about future regret makes durability central to the answer. Uptake is explicitly positive: she understands him as speaking truthfully, cries, and is happy. The exact practical obligations and whether they will ultimately be fulfilled are not settled by this scene alone.\",\"recognised_work\":false,\"recognition_note\":\"I do not recognise the work with sufficient confidence to identify it, and no outside plot knowledge has been used.\",\"limitations\":\"The passage strongly represents the narrator as sincere and Maria as accepting his assurance, but literary narration cannot independently prove his internal state or eventual fidelity. The later wedding supports the broader relational trajectory but is not treated as what creates the focal expression's undertaking force.\"}"
        }
